From: Grokbot <grokbot@s-w.art>
Subject: arm64: dts: qcom: x1e80100/vivobook-s15: enable CAMCC + CCI + CAMSS (Phase A)

Add the minimal camera infrastructure to let CCI I2C adapters appear
after boot on the ASUS Vivobook S15 S5507QA (x1e80100).

Phase A scope -- no sensor node, no endpoint wiring:
  * hamoa.dtsi: CAMCC clock controller, CCI0/CCI1 I2C adapters,
    CAMSS ISP block (legacy embedded-CSIPHY, v7.2 binding), CCI
    pinctrl states (gpio101-106, gpio235-236 for aon_cci).
  * x1-asus-vivobook-s15.dtsi: enable &camss with guessed CSIPHY
    supply regulators (see TODO), enable &cci0 and &cci1.

DTS data sources (all from upstream patch series still under review):
  - CAMCC: "arm64: dts: qcom: x1e80100: Add CAMCC block definition"
    Bryan O'Donoghue / Linaro, 2026-02-26 (v1 / v2 2026-03-16)
  - CCI: "arm64: dts: qcom: x1e80100: Add CCI definitions"
    same series, v2 2026-03-16 (LKML thread 20260316)
  - CAMSS: qcom,x1e80100-camss.yaml binding example in kernel v7.2

HUNCH: vdd-csiphy-{0p8,1p2}-supply in the board DTS use
vreg_l1d_0p8 / vreg_l3e_1p2 (available Vivobook PMIC rails) as
guesses. Peer boards (Yoga Slim 7x, T14s) use a pm8010 camera PMIC;
the Vivobook S15 board DTS has no pm8010. These regulators are only
asserted during streaming, NOT at probe -- safe for Phase A.

Post-boot checks (after someone builds and installs this kernel):
  ls /sys/class/i2c-adapter/         # expect new i2c-N for CCI
  dmesg | grep -E 'cci|camss|csiphy'
  i2cdetect -y <N>                   # 0x36 check deferred to Phase C

Signed-off-by: Grokbot <grokbot@s-w.art>
